# Assumption Register

**Project:** Sales Volatility, Promotion Effectiveness & Forecasting Benchmark Review
**Status:** Canonical governance document. Complements the Decision Log.
**Purpose:** The Decision Log explains why a decision was made. This register explains what each decision — and the analysis built on it — depends on. Every assumption below is explicit; none is left implicit in a notebook or recommendation.

---

| ID | Assumption | Source (Notebook / Decision) | Why This Assumption Exists | Potential Impact if False | Likelihood | Severity | Mitigation | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A01 | The removed incomplete record does not bias the analysis | Notebook 01 / D01 | A single row with ten missing fields was excluded rather than imputed | If the missingness were tied to a broader, undetected pattern rather than an isolated end-of-history artifact, other silent gaps could exist elsewhere in the dataset | Low | Low | Full-dataset missingness check performed in Notebook 01 found no other gap of any kind | Accepted |
| A02 | Promotion and non-promotion observations are independent enough for Mann-Whitney U | Notebook 03 / D02 | Mann-Whitney assumes independent observations; daily sales for the same SKU-store over time carry some autocorrelation, so independence is an approximation, not an exact property of this data | If autocorrelation is material, the reported p-value could be somewhat optimistic (appearing more significant than warranted), though the effect size (rank-biserial) would be less affected | Medium | Medium | Effect size reported alongside p-value throughout Notebook 03, specifically so a conclusion does not rest on the p-value alone | Needs Monitoring |
| A03 | Daily observations represent normal business operations | Notebook 01 / D01 | Stock-out days and the one discontinued SKU-store combination (SKU0004 × STORE0002) are atypical by definition | If atypical days were not correctly identified and handled, they would distort volatility and forecast-accuracy measures | Low | Low | Stock-out flag and discontinuation period explicitly identified and handled separately in Notebooks 01 and 03 | Accepted |
| A04 | Seasonality remains relatively stable across the observed three-year period | Notebook 03 (H4) / Notebook 04 (forecast baseline) | The Seasonal Historical Average and the seasonal hypothesis test both implicitly treat "June" in 2021, 2022, and 2023 as comparable | If the business or market shifted materially over the three years (new competitor, changed assortment, pricing strategy change), pooling all three years together would blur a real shift into a single averaged pattern | Medium | Medium | Not currently mitigated — no year-over-year stability check has been performed | Needs Monitoring |
| A05 | Weather measurements reasonably represent store operating conditions | Notebook 03 (H5) | Temperature and rainfall are recorded at some reference point (e.g., city-level), not necessarily at the store's exact location | If the weather data is a poor proxy for conditions actually experienced near the store, the "no measurable effect" finding could reflect a measurement gap rather than a genuine absence of a weather effect | Medium | Low | Finding is stated narrowly ("no measurable effect using these two weather measures"), not as a general claim about weather | Accepted |
| A06 | The two stores are analyzed only as these two specific stores; no conclusion is generalized beyond them | Notebooks 01, 03, 05 / D06 | Store, country, city, and channel are structurally confounded 1:1 in this dataset (one store per country per city per channel) | If a future reader generalizes a store-level finding to "channel type" or "market" in general, a conclusion would be drawn that this data cannot support | Low (within this project) / higher risk of downstream misuse | Critical if violated by a downstream reader | Explicit scope statement repeated in every notebook and in the README | Accepted |
| A07 | Missing cost data prevents ROI estimation; ROI is therefore intentionally excluded | Notebooks 01, 05 / D09 | No holding-cost, capital-cost, or promotion-spend field exists anywhere in the source dataset | None — this is a data-availability constraint, not a probabilistic assumption; the risk lies in a reader assuming ROI could be inferred indirectly | Low (confirmed absent) | Medium | Explicit "cannot be estimated" statements throughout Notebook 05 rather than a proxy estimate | Accepted |
| A08 | Historical data quality is sufficient after Notebook 01 cleaning | Notebook 01 | All subsequent notebooks build on the audited dataset without re-auditing | If the Notebook 01 audit missed an issue, every downstream statistic and forecast would inherit it silently | Low | Medium | Comprehensive audit (missingness, duplicates, type validation, date integrity, structural and numeric consistency checks) documented in Notebook 01 | Accepted |
| A09 | Seasonal Historical Average is an appropriate first forecasting benchmark | Notebook 04 / D07 | Chosen deliberately as the simplest, most explainable method before considering more complex alternatives | If the true underlying demand pattern has a component this method cannot capture (e.g., a trend), reported accuracy would be a floor, not a ceiling — understating what a more capable method could achieve | Low | Low | Explicitly framed as a benchmark, not a final forecasting system, throughout Notebook 04 | Accepted |
| A10 | The forecast benchmark is evaluated independently from planner judgement | Notebook 04 | No historical planner forecast data exists to compare against | A reader could mistakenly interpret the benchmark's 28.63% WAPE as "better than current planning" without any evidence for that comparison | Medium (risk of misinterpretation) | Medium | Notebook 04 and Notebook 05 both explicitly label this comparison "Insufficient Data" and recommend prospective logging to resolve it | Pending Validation |
| A11 | Recommendation confidence depends only on verified statistical evidence | Notebook 05 / D10 | Governance rule adopted to prevent an attractive but unevidenced idea from being recommended with the same confidence as a tested one | If not enforced consistently, a recommendation could carry more confidence than its underlying evidence supports | Low (self-imposed and checked) | High if violated | Explicit evidence-strength labeling and a dedicated "Recommendations NOT Supported" section in Notebook 05 | Accepted |
| A12 | Absence of statistical significance is not interpreted as evidence of no effect | Notebook 03 (H2, H5) | A "not supported" result (post-promotion dip, weather) means the specific test performed did not detect an effect — not that no effect could ever exist under any test | A reader could over-generalize "not supported" into "proven absent," particularly for weather (only two measures, same-day only, tested) and the post-promotion dip (only a 3-day window tested) | Medium | Medium | Findings phrased as "no evidence of X was found in this test," with the tested scope stated explicitly, rather than "X does not exist" | Needs Monitoring |

